“Jenkins Shared Library is a way to centralize and reuse common Jenkins pipeline code across multiple projects. 
Instead of maintaining the same Jenkinsfile logic in every application repository, we keep reusable Groovy functions 
in a centralized Git repository and import them into Jenkins pipelines.”

“In my projects, we used Shared Libraries for common CI/CD activities such as Git checkout, Maven builds, SonarQube
analysis, Docker image building, Trivy scanning, pushing images to ECR, and Kubernetes deployment.”

“For example, we had a common buildDockerImage() function and deployToEKS() function in the shared library. Application 
teams only needed a small Jenkinsfile and passed parameters such as application name, Docker tag, ECR repository, and
Kubernetes namespace.”

“This helped us avoid duplicate pipeline code, maintain standard CI/CD practices, and make changes centrally. If we 
needed to modify the Docker scanning or deployment logic, we could update the shared library instead of changing every 
application's Jenkinsfile.”

